"""Rebuilt executive summary. ops in document order.
('rw', pid, text) rewrite in place (word diff); ('rep', pid, text) delete old paragraph and insert new one after it;
('del', pid); ('new', after_pid, style_like_pid, text)."""
Q = "how can the Western Cape grow international arrivals from about 1.5 million to 2.5 million by 2035, when interest far exceeds visits and seats pace growth at the peak?"
ES = [
 ('rw', '14B01311', "International visitors have returned to the Western Cape in record numbers. About 1.57 million arrived through Cape Town International in FY25/26, 35% more than in FY19/20. The recovery is complete. Growth is the harder task. This plan sets a target of 2.5 million international arrivals by 2035. Reaching it depends first on turning interest into visits, and then on seats at the summer peak."),
 ('del', '41D8289A'), ('del', '1C9A62FB'),
 ('rw', '02FD9A23', "The Western Cape earns more per visitor than the rest of South Africa. Its foreign visitors spend roughly 1.6 times the national average: about US$890 per arrival, against US$500 for the rest of South Africa. Foreign direct spend rose 14% in a year, from R22.7 billion in FY2024 to R25.9 billion in FY2025."),
 ('rw', '560EEDA1', "The premium rests on a few markets, six months and one metro. Five markets supply 63% of overseas arrivals, and the UK, Germany and the USA alone 52%. October to March carries between 61% and 62% of overseas visitor travel. The Cape Metro holds 40.3% of the province's 50,332 tourism assets."),
 ('del', '2FA3DC05'),
 ('rep', '62351992', "However, the province converts little of the interest it attracts. About 97 million high-value travellers in 34 markets are interested in South Africa, and about 1.4 million arrive. Six barriers stand between interest and a visit: long-haul distance and cost, safety perceptions, no reason to travel now, reluctance to self-drive, low awareness beyond the core and difficulty finding information. The barriers apply all year and in every market."),
 ('del', '347BF66D'),
 ('rep', '2A22EA68', "Seats pace growth at the summer peak. Eleven of 27 international routes run above an 80% load factor, and one main runway caps movements at about 30 an hour. Airports Company South Africa (ACSA) schedules the realigned runway for December 2029, subject to contract award. The plan assumes about 2033 and counts the earlier date as upside. In the green season, April to September, seats and visitors fall away together: June holds 6.4% of the year's international capacity, against 10.8% in December."),
 ('del', '18874EA2'), ('del', '19E04ED5'),
 ('rep', '4D3CFAC6', "Reach and mandate limit the rest. Accessibility falls from 4.7 out of 5 in the Cape Metro to 1.0 in the Central Karoo, and beyond the Metro and the Garden Route the province is effectively car-only. National government and its state-owned entities hold visas, air service agreements and the airport and port programmes. Entry moved to the Electronic Travel Authorisation (ETA) in August 2026, and no performance data is published yet. Indian and Chinese arrivals to South Africa fell 30.0% and 23.7% in the first nine months of 2026."),
 ('del', '376D5D04'),
 ('rep', '521EC8B7', "The study behind this plan set out to find a viable growth target for international arrivals to 2035. It answers one question: " + Q + ""),
 ('del', '781819CF'),
 ('rep', '0206FD3C', "The growth model draws three cases from a 2025 base of about 1.5 million arrivals. By 2035 the conservative case reaches about 1.82 million, the base case about 2.18 million and the accelerated case about 2.52 million. The target is the accelerated case, rounded."),
 ('rw', '612D97B9', "The cases differ by intervention, not by demand potential. The base case assumes Wesgro holds its current effort while Emirates, Qatar, Ethiopian and Turkish Airlines keep adding capacity. The accelerated case also needs direct India and China routes and dedicated activation in India, China, the USA and the Gulf. Without confirmed routes, it reverts to base."),
 ('rw', '2068628F', "Figure ES.1. Arrivals by case to 2035"),
 ('del', '4A627B3D'),
 ('rep', '3F1EBA1D', "At an 80% load factor the target requires about 3.1 million inbound seats a year by 2035, about 1.2 million more than in the year to November 2025. The base case implies growth of 3.8% a year and the accelerated case 5.3%, against a global norm of 3% to 5%."),
 ('rw', '7FC5C4BC', "In Goal 1, Reshape demand, the work moves from awareness alone to conversion against the six barriers. Campaigns carry dated reasons to travel and current safety information. Regional itineraries, transfers and practical information go onto the platforms each market uses. Hub traffic already in the air is converted: about 101,000 air journeys a year from India and China reach Cape Town, mainly through Gulf and African hubs."),
 ('rw', '61197015', "In Goal 2, Transform supply, the work moves from new attractions to seats and finished experiences. Initiative I6 scales the existing Cape Town Air Access partnership to win frequency, larger aircraft and winter seats on the full routes ahead of runway relief, and does not create a fund. At the flagship sites the task is to manage flows. Elsewhere it is to turn existing assets into complete visitor experiences."),
 ('rw', '27EDC559', "In Goal 3, Build enabling capability, the work moves from separate mandates to shared decisions, capital and data. Twenty township and rural communities hold 18.4% of the province's residents, but the spend traced to them is just 0.02% to 0.04% of foreign direct spend. The capital pipeline funds airports, ports, roads and rail, but almost none of it reaches the beds, booking and product the regions lack. More than twenty bodies produce tourism data, yet no one holds a single view of visitor behaviour."),
 ('del', '67B8BA80'),
 ('rw', '19C20F98', "The plan is Wesgro's, and Wesgro cannot bind other bodies. Each initiative names the stakeholders it needs, and its first deliverable is their agreement on who is accountable for what (section 4.4)."),
 ('rw', '51DAE427', "The plan sets three goals, one for each shift, and twenty objectives beneath them in six themes (Figure ES.2). Eighteen initiatives deliver the objectives. Chapter 3 gives each objective's evidence, actions and stakeholders, and Appendix A1 traces each to its sources."),
 ('rep', '2A84D83C', "Conversion and seat retention move first, because every other lever runs through them. Phase 1, Activate, runs from 2027 to 2028. In the first half of 2027 Wesgro convenes each initiative's stakeholders and, with the Department of Economic Development and Tourism (DEDAT), sets up the Tourism Growth Steerco. It then tables the first national escalation agenda and agrees the data-sharing protocol. Phase 2, Expand, runs from 2029 to 2031 and scales what the first phase proved. Phase 3, Sustain, runs from 2032 to 2035. It takes direct route cases to carriers as the international terminal (about 2032) and the realigned runway (about 2033) add capacity."),
 ('rw', '22BFA5FA', "Decision gates close the first two phases: Gate 1 at the end of 2028 and Gate 2 at the end of 2031. A mid-term review in 2030 tests seat growth against the required path, and a terminal evaluation reports in 2035. Eleven of the fourteen actions for the first 24 months sit inside Wesgro's current mandate and budget. Dates are proposals (section 4.5)."),
 ('rw', '4F302046', "Progress is tracked at three levels: eight impact indicators, thirty-one outcome indicators and forty-one output indicators. Arrivals are the first measure of success, not the last."),
 ('rw', '0452EA29', "Table ES.1. Impact indicators and targets"),
 ('rw', '7BEDDA5E', "Arrivals carry targets in both cases, while jobs and GDP contribution wait on the economic measures workstream and season, spread and community spend start from dated baselines."),
 ('rw', '58BEB9C3', "The airport count is tracked beside the model's arrivals baseline (section 5.3). The spend target needs the same care. The model's 2024 spend of R27.12 billion sits 19% above the R22.7 billion reported for FY2024, so the spend path is to be rebased on the FY2025 actual."),
 ('rw', '27E95CCF', "Jobs and GDP contribution have no target yet. A named workstream in initiative I16, Tourism economic measures, sets provincial measures of both in 2027, and Gate 1 turns the new baselines into targets. The 300,000 tourism-linked jobs ambition is tested against that measure before it becomes a target."),
 ('rw', '3E9A4B6A', "Funding and risks"),
 ('new', '3E9A4B6A', '404BEFA9', "No initiative has been costed [[QUERY Q1: cost of the plan and who pays?]]. Thirteen of the eighteen initiatives draw mainly on Wesgro's operating budget. Four need private capital as a main source, three of them alongside that budget. The capital the plan depends on sits with ACSA, the Passenger Rail Agency of South Africa (PRASA), Transnet and private investors. Rand bands are still to be set with Wesgro Finance and DEDAT, with a stop list of the Wesgro work deprioritised to fund the initiatives (section 4.6)."),
 ('rw', '404BEFA9', "Twelve of the sixteen material risks sit outside Wesgro's control. Two are rated high on both likelihood and impact. Restrictive bilateral air agreements and the US carrier cap call for an escalation agenda with stated asks, plus route work inside existing rights. Gulf hub disruption, which occurred in 2026, calls for hub work spread beyond the Gulf and resilience conditions in Cape Town Air Access route support. Six more are rated medium likelihood and high impact, among them a slip in runway relief past about 2033 and a cut to Wesgro's operating budget (section 4.8)."),
 ('del', '3C35EB2E'),
 ('rep', '7B8C13CE', "The Western Cape does not lack interest. Conversion, seats at the summer peak and reach beyond the Metro hold it back. Reaching 2.5 million arrivals by 2035 depends on conversion work that starts in 2027 and on seats held on the full routes until the runway brings relief. Most of the first two years' work sits inside Wesgro's mandate. The rest starts with agreement."),
]
# Table ES.4 (caption 1AFA7412 and the table after it) is deleted; Tables ES.1 to ES.3 move to Chapter 3.
